 and uncertainty stays elevated", "Term-limited Republican presidency gives Democrats the historical out-party tailwind"], "rationale": "Massachusetts' durable double-digit Democratic lean anchors the forecast, and the open seat offers Republicans no incumbent offset. With no state polls or expert ratings available, fundamentals carry the call, keeping uncertainty wide but the Democratic nominee clearly favored.", "watch": "If either party nominates a candidate with unusual cross-party appeal in Massachusetts, or early state polling shows a materially different race, the margin could compress or widen.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Massachusetts remains one of the most Democratic states in presidential voting, and the open-seat nature of this race does little to change that. No incumbent is on the ballot, and no candidate-specific polling or expert ratings exist yet, so the forecast rests on the state's partisan fundamentals. Recent cycles have produced Democratic margins well into double digits, and Republicans have not carried the state at the presidential level in decades.\n\nThe main uncertainty is candidate-specific: the eventual nominees are unknown, and a moderate Republican with statewide appeal could run closer than the typical national ticket, while a polarizing one could push the margin wider. With no polls to anchor a state-specific estimate, uncertainty stays elevated. The race is rated Likely Democratic, with the Democratic nominee clearly favored but not yet at safe levels.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9396, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8954, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8937, "margin": 14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8913, "margin":